Pin junos-eznc below 2.8.1 for IPv6 Juniper devices

junos-eznc 2.8.1 added hostname validation that rejects bare IPv6
literals as the `host` value, but napalm's junos driver (5.1.0) passes
the device IP through to jnpr.junos.Device() unbracketed. This breaks
both PortAdmin and the SeedDB connectivity check for any Juniper device
registered by an IPv6 address.

This is a Debian-only stopgap carried until the fix lands in an upstream
NAV release; drop this patch once constraints.txt upstream pins the same
range (NAV 5.19.1+). See Uninett/nav#4119.

--- nav-debian.orig/constraints.txt
+++ nav-debian/constraints.txt
@@ -1,3 +1,9 @@
 # Bound the transitive paramiko range so pip-compile's backtracking resolver
 # doesn't enumerate versions across napalm/junos-eznc/ncclient/netmiko.
 paramiko>=4,<5
+
+# junos-eznc 2.8.1 added hostname validation that rejects bare IPv6 literals,
+# but napalm's junos driver hands them the device IP unbracketed. This breaks
+# PortAdmin and SeedDB for IPv6-addressed Juniper devices. Pin below the
+# offending release until napalm's junos driver brackets IPv6 hosts. See #4119.
+junos-eznc<2.8.1
